\section*{Limitations}\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Single dataset.}
All probe experiments use one dataset (HarmThoughts~\citep{harmthoughts}); the geometric precondition (shallow-layer compression) is confirmed on AdvBench~\citep{advbench} (\cref{app:cosine_volatility,app:advbench_cp}), but CP-dependent claims rely on HarmThoughts, which provides step-level CP annotations derived from manual inspection of reasoning chains. The dataset spans multiple harmful categories (e.g., violence, fraud, illegal activities) but we do not stratify results by category; categories with longer average reasoning chains or earlier CPs may exhibit different probe dynamics. CP positions range from early to late within traces, though the distribution is right-skewed (most CPs occur in the first half of reasoning). Annotation granularity (step-level vs.\ token-level), category distribution, and annotator team composition may all influence which layers carry discriminative signal and how strongly metric bias manifests. Inter-annotator agreement (IAA) was not computed for these annotations, though an LLM-based proxy on AdvBench achieved $\kappa{=}0.606$ (\cref{app:iaa}). Random annotation noise would attenuate effect sizes symmetrically, making our estimates conservative; systematic noise (e.g., consistent early or late CP placement) could bias layer selection or inflate modality differences. Without IAA, we cannot distinguish these cases, so our conclusions may not transfer to independently annotated corpora. Generalization to other jailbreak datasets, attack taxonomies, or multilingual settings is untested.

\paragraph{Model-specific caveats and statistical power.}
Current probes characterize the commitment phenomenon rather than constitute production detectors: false positive rates remain 11--50\% (model-dependent), and the 76.7\% oracle gap (R1-8B; \cref{sec:oracle}) underscores how much room remains. R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ (25\% coverage, 5/20 traces) and its HS advantage shows a pattern consistent with position mediation (residualized gap +1.9pp, $p{=}0.914$; \cref{app:position_residual}). QwQ-32B's position baseline achieves higher balanced accuracy (0.832 vs.\ 0.796) with negative HS lead time. R1-8B FPR significance is seed-fragile (3/5 HB). At $n{=}19$--$20$, the minimum detectable effect at 80\% power is 8.6--9.5pp (\cref{app:power_analysis}); all 32B conclusions marked $\dagger$ should be interpreted with this constraint. A text-feature extraction bug, discovered and corrected during development (\cref{app:bug}), affected intermediate QwQ-32B results but not the final analysis.

\paragraph{Hyperparameter selection and scope.}
Layer (up to 64), $W$ (7 values), $K$ (5 values), and threshold are selected on the same validation set ($n{=}4$--$9$ per-class at 32B, up to $2{,}240$ configurations). The 12-test HB boundary covers final model-metric comparisons but not the full upstream exploration; Type~I error may be underestimated. HB correction covers the final 12 model-metric tests but not the cumulative researcher degrees of freedom in layer, metric, window, and evidence tier selection; cumulative degrees of freedom are substantial (\cref{app:hyperparameter_robustness} provides convergence, cross-validation, and bootstrap analyses addressing this concern). BA-optimal layer indices are sensitive to the train/validation split, spanning 7--98\% depth across models and splits (\cref{app:ba_layer_comparison}); for example, OT-7B shifts from L16 to L2 and QwQ-32B from L60 to L63 between 70/30 and 60/20/20 splits, a direct manifestation of the layer selection sensitivity that constitutes our primary finding. Threshold-based metrics are more stable but still vary by up to 4 layers. $K{=}5$ was selected on the R1-8B validation set and held fixed (\cref{app:k_ablation}); joint (layer, $W$) stability was verified for QwQ-32B (\cref{app:layer_sweep}) but not R1-32B. CP annotations follow HarmThoughts~\citep{harmthoughts} without independent IAA evaluation; if annotation error is ${\geq}2$ steps, QwQ-32B's lead time ($-$1.2 to +1.9) falls within noise, though perturbation analysis shows smooth degradation with no cliff effects (\cref{app:cp_perturbation}). Three of four models share the Qwen architecture family; observed patterns may reflect Qwen-specific representation properties rather than universal reasoning-model characteristics. Only R1-8B (Llama-based) provides evidence from a second architecture family. We cover 2 architectures and 2 scales; whether metric-induced layer selection bias generalizes to non-reasoning LLMs or encoder-only architectures remains an open question. Intermediate scales, diverse base architectures, proprietary models, and multilingual settings are untested. Results are correlational; causal intervention is needed to confirm mechanistic involvement. Cross-model transfer fails entirely (\cref{app:transfer_details}), suggesting safety signals are model-specific geometric configurations; future work must develop model-specific probes or identify shared safety subspaces via more sophisticated alignment (e.g., Procrustes rotation, CKA-guided projection~\citep{kornblith2019cka}, model stitching~\citep{bansal2021stitching}).

\paragraph{CP permanence assumption.}
Our binary CP definition assumes that once safety deliberation ceases, it does not resume. In practice, some reasoning traces exhibit oscillating patterns where the model intermittently returns to safety-aware reasoning after apparent commitment. The current binary framework treats the first permanent cessation as CP, but traces with oscillating safety deliberation violate this assumption and may be misclassified. A multi-state formulation (e.g., pre-commitment / wavering / committed) could better capture these dynamics but would require richer annotations and larger datasets to train reliably.

\section*{Ethics Statement}
This work analyzes safety-relevant reasoning traces to characterize probe behavior, not to circumvent safety mechanisms. All experiments use publicly available models and datasets (HarmThoughts, AdvBench). No human subjects were involved. Code and scripts for all experiments and analyses will be released upon acceptance.
